---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base
  title="Lutaro - Camera Setup Guide"
  description="How to set up your Sony camera for Lutaro: Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, PC Remote, Access Authentication, pairing, and USB on iPhone, iPad, and Mac. With the exact menu path for every setting."
>
  <h1>Set up your Sony camera for Lutaro</h1>
  <p class="meta">Menu paths verified against Sony's official α6700 Help Guide · July 2026</p>

  <p>
    Lutaro connects to your camera in the same way as Sony's own software:
    over Wi-Fi using the camera's <strong>PC Remote</strong> setting, or
    through a USB cable. Both work on iPhone, iPad, and Mac. This guide
    shows every camera setting you need, with the exact menu path for each.
  </p>
  <p>
    To see which cameras work, and what each one supports, go to the <a
    href={`${base}/compatibility/`}>camera list</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Two sets of menu names.</strong> Newer cameras (a9&nbsp;III,
    α1&nbsp;II, ZV-E1, and recent firmware on the a7&nbsp;IV, a7R&nbsp;V,
    and α1) renamed two menus: <em>Cnct./PC&nbsp;Remote → PC&nbsp;Remote
    Function</em> is now <em>Cnct./Remote&nbsp;Sht. → Remote Shoot
    Function</em>. The α6700 and older cameras keep the old names. This
    guide shows both. Every other menu name is the same.
  </div>

  <h2 id="wifi">1. Put the camera on your Wi-Fi network</h2>
  <p>
    Camera and device (iPhone, iPad, or Mac) must be on the same Wi-Fi
    network.
  </p>
  <span class="path">MENU → Network → Wi-Fi → Wi-Fi Connect = On</span>
  <p>
    The camera searches for networks. If yours is not listed, use
    <strong>WPS Push</strong> (press the WPS button on your router), or
    <strong>Access Point Set.</strong> to type the network name and password
    by hand. Both are in the same Wi-Fi menu.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    Recent firmware (α6700 2.00 and later) works only with WPA2 and WPA3
    networks. The camera will not join older WPA or WEP networks.
  </div>

  <h2 id="away-from-home">2. Connecting away from home</h2>
  <p>
    No router nearby? Lutaro can still connect. The easiest way is
    Bluetooth: Lutaro asks the camera to start its own hotspot, then joins
    it for you. If nothing else works, you can always type the camera's IP
    address by hand.
  </p>

  <h3 id="bluetooth">Bluetooth (easiest): Lutaro starts the camera's hotspot</h3>
  <p>
    On iPhone and iPad, Lutaro can use Bluetooth to tell the camera to start
    its own Wi-Fi hotspot, then connect through it. This is how Sony's
    Creators' App connects too. Set it up once on the camera:
  </p>
  <span class="path">MENU → Network → Bluetooth → Bluetooth Function = On</span>
  <span class="path">MENU → Network → Bluetooth → Pairing</span>
  <p>
    Open <em>Pairing</em> on the camera. Then start the Bluetooth connection
    in Lutaro and confirm the message on the camera screen. You can also let
    Lutaro <em>wake</em> a camera that is switched off:
  </p>
  <span class="path">MENU → Network → Cnct./PC Remote → Cnct. while Power OFF = On</span>
  <div class="note">
    When you connect through the camera's own hotspot, leave <em>Wi-Fi
    Connect</em> (step 1) <strong>off</strong>. The hotspot needs it off. A
    camera woken by Bluetooth starts in hotspot mode. It will not join your
    home Wi-Fi until you turn that on yourself.
  </div>

  <h3>Camera as its own access point (manual)</h3>
  <p>No Bluetooth pairing? Turn the camera into a Wi-Fi access point from its own menu and join it directly:</p>
  <span class="path">MENU → Network → Cnct./PC Remote → PC Remote Function → Wi-Fi Direct Info</span>
  <p>
    That screen shows the camera's own network name and password. Join it
    from your iPhone's Wi-Fi settings. In this mode the camera is always at
    <strong>192.168.122.1</strong>, and Lutaro finds it there automatically.
  </p>

  <h3>iPhone Personal Hotspot</h3>
  <p>
    Turn on Personal Hotspot on your iPhone and keep the Personal Hotspot
    screen open so the camera can find it, then join it from the camera:
  </p>
  <span class="path">MENU → Network → Wi-Fi → Access Point Set.</span>
  <p>
    The default iPhone hotspot settings work on the α6700. Nothing to
    change.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <strong>Camera can't see the hotspot?</strong> Set
    <span class="path">MENU → Network → Wi-Fi → Wi-Fi Frequency Band</span>
    to 2.4&nbsp;GHz on the camera. If your iPhone has <em>Maximize
    Compatibility</em> in the Personal Hotspot settings, turn that on too.
  </div>

  <h3>Manual IP entry</h3>
  <p>
    This works in every mode, and is what to try if a scan does not find the
    camera. When the camera is its own access point, the address is always
    <strong>192.168.122.1</strong>. On a hotspot or a shared network, look
    up the camera's current IP address in its own network settings screen.
    Then enter it in Lutaro under <strong>Enter IP Manually</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pc-remote">3. Turn on PC Remote</h2>
  <p>This is the switch that lets Lutaro connect at all.</p>
  <div class="overflow">
    <table>
      <tr><th>Bodies</th><th>Menu path</th></tr>
      <tr>
        <td>α6700 and most earlier bodies</td>
        <td>MENU → Network → Cnct./PC Remote → PC Remote Function →
            PC Remote = <strong>On</strong></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>a9 III, α1 II, ZV-E1, updated a7 IV / a7R V / α1</td>
        <td>MENU → Network → Cnct./Remote Sht. → Remote Shoot Function →
            Remote Shooting = <strong>On</strong></td>
      </tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    The camera turns off Wi-Fi when it sleeps. If Lutaro cannot find it,
    wake the camera by half-pressing the shutter button, then scan again.
  </div>

  <h2 id="access-auth">4. Access Authentication - with or without a password</h2>
  <p>
    Sony cameras can protect remote connections with a user name and
    password. The setting is called <strong>Access Authen.</strong> It is in
    the same place on both menu versions:
  </p>
  <span class="path">MENU → Network → Network Option → Access Authen. Settings → Access Authen.</span>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>On</strong> - the connection is encrypted. Lutaro asks for the
    camera's user name and password. Sony recommends this.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Off</strong> - no password and no encryption. Instead you
    approve each new device once on the camera (<a
    href="#pairing">pairing</a>, below). Sony warns that other people can
    read this connection.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Where to find the user name and password</h3>
  <span class="path">MENU → Network → Network Option → Access Authen. Info</span>
  <p>
    This screen shows the camera's user name, password, MAC address, and
    fingerprint. The camera creates these at the factory. You can change
    them, or make a new password, under <em>Access Authen. Settings</em>.
    Type the user name and password into Lutaro exactly as shown.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    If you make a new password, saved connections in Lutaro will fail until
    you enter it. The app tells you when this happens.
  </div>

  <h2 id="pairing">5. Pairing (only when Access Authen. is Off)</h2>
  <p>
    With Access Authen. <strong>Off</strong>, you must approve the first
    connection from each new device on the camera itself:
  </p>
  <div class="overflow">
    <table>
      <tr><th>Bodies</th><th>Menu path</th></tr>
      <tr>
        <td>α6700 and most earlier bodies</td>
        <td>MENU → Network → Cnct./PC Remote → PC Remote Function → Pairing</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>a9 III, α1 II, ZV-E1, updated a7 IV / a7R V / α1</td>
        <td>MENU → Network → Cnct./Remote Sht. → Remote Shoot Function → Pairing</td>
      </tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <em>Pairing</em> on the camera.</li>
    <li>Connect from Lutaro.</li>
    <li>Approve the prompt that appears on the camera screen.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The camera remembers up to 20 paired devices. If the list is full, or
    after you reset the camera, clear it and pair again:
  </p>
  <span class="path">MENU → Network → Network Option → Reset Network Set.</span>
  <div class="note">
    Reset Network Set. clears <em>all</em> network settings, including saved
    Wi-Fi networks. You will need to do step 1 again.
  </div>

  <h2 id="usb">6. USB cable</h2>
  <p>
    Lutaro can connect over a USB cable instead of Wi-Fi, on iPhone, iPad,
    and Mac. No network, no pairing, and no password. Set the camera's USB
    mode to remote control:
  </p>
  <span class="path">MENU → Setup → USB → USB Connection Mode → PC Remote</span>
  <p>
    If USB Connection Mode is set to <em>Sel. When Connect</em>, the camera
    asks every time you plug in the cable. Choose <strong>Remote Shoot (PC
    Remote)</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    Use a <strong>data</strong> cable, not a charging-only cable. On iPhone
    or iPad you need a USB-C port or a USB-C camera adapter. Steps 1, 4, and
    5 (Wi-Fi, Access Authen., pairing) do not apply over a cable. The <a
    href={`${base}/compatibility/`}>camera list</a> shows which cameras work
    over USB.
  </div>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">7. If something does not work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Camera not found on Wi-Fi</strong> - it is probably asleep,
    because the camera turns off Wi-Fi when idle. Wake it, check it is on
    the same network, and check that PC Remote (step 3) is On.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Scan finds nothing away from home</strong> - with no local
      network, Lutaro shows a "No Local Network" screen and still checks any
      recently-connected camera. The simplest fix is a
      <a href="#usb">USB cable</a>, which needs no network at all. Otherwise
      use one of the four <a href="#away-from-home">away-from-home options</a>:
      Bluetooth, camera-as-access-point, Personal Hotspot, or manual IP entry.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Camera is busy"</strong> - another app is already connected.
    Close Imaging Edge Desktop, Creators' App, or any other Sony remote
    software, then try again. A Sony camera allows only one connection at a
    time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wrong user name or password</strong> - check them again on the
    camera under <em>Network → Network Option → Access Authen. Info</em>.
    Capital letters matter.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Fingerprint changed"</strong> - this appears after you reset
    the camera or its network settings, or if a different camera answers at
    the same address. If you did reset the camera, or this really is another
    camera, accept the new fingerprint. If not, check your network.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No pairing message on the camera</strong> - open the
    <em>Pairing</em> menu (step 5) on the camera <em>before</em> you connect
    from Lutaro.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Camera will not wake over Bluetooth</strong> - you need all
    three: <em>Bluetooth Function</em> On, the camera paired with this phone
    (<a href="#bluetooth">above</a>), and <em>Cnct. while Power OFF</em> On.
    If the Bluetooth connection keeps failing at once, the pairing may be
    out of date. Forget the camera in your iPhone's Bluetooth settings, then
    pair again from <em>Network → Bluetooth → Pairing</em>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cannot join the camera's hotspot</strong> - look for a Wi-Fi
    message on your phone and allow it. If your phone cannot see the hotspot
    at all, set <em>Network → Wi-Fi → Wi-Fi Frequency Band</em> to
    2.4&nbsp;GHz.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="support">Questions?</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:lutaro@akitaka.me">lutaro@akitaka.me</a>, or open an
    issue at
    <a href="https://github.com/tarakanof/Lutaro-Site/issues">github.com/tarakanof/Lutaro-Site/issues</a>.
    Both routes are on the <a href={`${base}/support/`}>support page</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="meta" style="margin-top: 3rem; font-size: 0.85em;">
    The menu names are Sony's. They were checked against the official <a
    href="https://helpguide.sony.net/ilc/2320/v1/en/">α6700 Help Guide</a>
    in July 2026, and against the a9&nbsp;III, α1&nbsp;II, and ZV-E1 guides
    for the newer names. Sony may rename menus in future firmware. If a path
    does not match your camera, check the <a
    href="https://www.sony.net/electronics/support/">help guide for your
    model</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
